>2000, Boing Boing and to a lesser extent, Wired.  All are slick, high
>distribution magazines.  Is there a large scale trend in the making?  (If
>this has been covered, pardon me, I'm new here!)

It reminds me mostly of the similar burst of science and high technology
magazines circa 1980, probably triggered by the appearance of "Omni" in
1978.

Besides "Omni," new mags included "Science '80," "Discover," a newly
revamped "Science Digest," and my all-time favorite "High Technology."

"High Technology" started out with a slightly different name, but really
hit its stride in the mid-80s, with spectacular articles on all kinds of
advanced technologies. I have saved every issue, and also picked up free
duplicate copies at libraries, just to have these priceless reference
articles.

Sadly, ownership changed hands and the editorial focus shifted to "business
technology" (you know, where the title becomes something crappy like "High
Technology: The magazine of enterprise-wide networked systems"). And just
after I bought a 3-year subscription for $60, despite the changed focus (I
still had hopes). they ceased publication and gave me a 5-year (ugh!)
subscription to "Success!" magazine, which is apparently filled with ads
for self-improvement tapes, minor business tricks, "it worked for me!"
testimonials, and the usual Tony Robbins NLP crap.

And "Science '86" was the last incarnation of that mag, "Science Digest" is
no more, and "Omni" has gone even further downhill and looks to be for
sale.

But there's good news. My other favorite, "Discover," got bought by the
Disney company and seems to have a long life ahead of it. Where else can
you find cover stories about Kip Thorne's time travel ideas?

Back to the question about "Mondo 2000," "Wired," etc. I predict the public
will reach saturation on these ideas and will tire. Some of these mags will
fail. There's a limit to how many times you can run articles on Durk and
Sandy, on aluminum foil halter tops passing as high tech fashion, on
cyberpunk cowboys, or even on crypto rebels :-} . 


Whether "Extropy" can succeed is up to us.

-Tim May
