> In April 1979, Kosta Tsipis published the lead article (also known as the
> "technology policy" article) in "Sci Am," an article on "Particle Beam
> Weapons." Tsipis "proved" they could never be built with the following
> general reasoning (I don't have the article handly, so these numbers are
> rough guesses, to show the line of reasoning):

[parlor-trick dismissal of ABM DEW elided]

> A pretty devastating critique of particle beam weapons!
> 
> Except for what he missed.... which is where I enter the picture.

[how Tsipis blew it elided]

While Tsipis was taking his usual line ("war is bad, so any means of
waging it more puissantly is to be discouraged"), here he's simply
guilty of staying within the bounds of conventional art.  He picked the
mechanism he did - penetrating the RV to cause structural failure if not
detonation of the chemical explosives in the warhead primary - for
feedback reasons, i.e., it's not enough to kill it, you have to _know_
you've killed it.  For example, Army doctrine for engaging tanks calls
for shooting until the tank burns, although it may have been out of
action some time before.  He thus took the most obvious method of
assessing gross damage; if I remember correctly, I think he may even have
mentioned the electronics-damage mechanism.

Still a straw man, but a more refined one...

