> The problem, I think, is that you are using your own private definition
> of Objectivism. You say that the epistemology is crappy, you also
> have said that her attempt to make morality absolute was a mistake,
> and you don't like her theory of romantic art.
> 
> What the hell is left that is *distinctive* of Objectivism? Nothing that
> I see. All that is left is egoism (sort of), classical liberalism,
> and a novel condemnation of altruism. Fine stuff, but not what most
> people who have read Rand call Objectivism.

I'd disagree. I'd say that the points about running one's life on
non-altruistic principles ARE new, at least in the form that Rand
phrased them. Many other classical liberal writers say things like
"well, charity is fine -- just not GOVERNMENT charity", but Rand fully
rejects this view. The objectivst ethics and politics admittedly
overlap with other peoples -- but then again, very little in politics
or ethics is ever new. What is distinctive about "Extropianism" by
your definition? After all, its just a mash of ideas that were
floating around. Whats distinctive about Lutheranism? Whats
distinctive about ANY philosophy if one requires that much novelty?

For the most part, she doesn't even get in to the worst aspects of her
philosophy in "Atlas" or "The Fountainhead", just in her non-fiction.
Most of her harangues in "Atlas" are fundamentally right, other than
the obsessive "A is A" stuff, which isn't even the bulk of Galt's
speech.

As for "not what most people who read Rand call Objectivism", I must
disagree there, too. For many of the people I know who've read Rand,
she is the first introduction they get to any of these ideas at all.
Also for most of those people, epistemology isn't even something they
care about -- its the support of Capitalism and rational egoism that
they get out of her, and that they like.

Perry

