Date: Fri, 30 Apr 1993 14:33:12 -0400 (EDT)
From: esr@snark.thyrsus.com (Eric S. Raymond)
Subject: TECH: Would `dark fiber' be vulnerable to jamming?

I was much interested by the article Harry forwarded on `dark fiber'.  The idea
that bandwidth could be used to replace switching technology, converting the
fiber net to a broadcast medium, in which *everybody* receives *everything*,
and simply selects what they want, is most intriguing.

However, the "dumbness" of the network (its lack of switching capacity) raises
an interesting question.  Is it critically vulnerable to jamming?

Here's the scenario.  It's 2020 and all-optical "dumb-fiber" networks have
taken over.  Like everyone else, I have a tap in my home.  It gives me instant
million-channel access to all the world's video on my wall-sized high-
definition TV, and gigabit-per-second digital connections to every machine on
the Internet (which is now worldwide and includes effectively every computer
made since the year 2000).

Comes the day I go psychotic from information overload and decide it's all
too much.  I pull the plug on my PC and shine a bright white light into the
connector.

*And the whole world network grinds to a halt!*

If transmission is cheap, jamming is too.  To hell with encoding; I just
drowned out all the carriers!  And, best of all, *they can't find me*!
There are no switches, no addressing as such.  *How are they going to know
where I am?*

Beyond the ease of jamming, there's another problem.

Frequency allocation in radio only works because broadcasting equipment
is expensive, and broadcasters easy to track.  Even so, where enforcement
is poor, "pirate radio" can be a problem.

How much worse is it going to get on a "dumb fiber" network, where (if I've
got it right) *you can't reserve a frequency*?  *Anyone* could grab a
given channel just by broadcasting on it.

Maybe the phone companies have a point...
-- 
