Date: Fri, 30 Apr 93 16:59:28 CDT
From: bsc835!mjohnson@uunet.uu.net
Subject: META: A Cooling Off Period?

 ... and then spake tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May): 

tm} Please, folks, we wouldn't be on this list if we didn't share
tm} some basic values and some basic interests in the future!
tm} Calling people fools, bigots, Communists, Randroids, etc., does
tm} not seem to be at all useful.

Something I believe we all know, yet much too easily forget, is the
tendency for virtual communications to acquire an aggressive vigor
in matters of ideological presentation and defense which seems to
far outstrip face to face communications, in both acceleration of
vehemence and in peak output of same.

It is my opinion that the roots of net.rudeness are twofold: 1) the
removal of the ever present face to face danger of a punch in the
nose (or other immediate physical harm) and 2) (more apropos for we
sensitive gentle beings :-) the removal of feedback which indicates
that non-physical harm has been done (tears, slumping of posture,
and other anxiety behavior) which would tend to modify our responses
in meat meeting relations.  I find myself wondering how such
flamefests would be preceived in a VR environ, and conclude that
recreational VR interactions would take on much the same flavors as
a hackwork "road warrior" late night cable movie but without the
clear demarcations of hero and villian (we are most all heros in our
own movies :-)  But anyway...

It's a pleasurable ego stroking thing to put forth your ideas and
see others accept them as their own.  Much more so if by dent of
persuasive ability you can correct someone else's "wrongful"
thinking and replace it with your own, more "rightful", memetic
processes.  Because, at the base of things, we are the kind of
animals that we are, this conceptual territorial expansion is a
"good thing", so we are compelled to propogate our individual
thoughtforms.  When others cannot see the clarity and desirability
of our ways of perception and fail to be converted by our arguments,
there is a tendency to wish to clearly brand the foe so that
onlookers might be less likely to accept memetic data from such an
"obviously" flawed source.

Flamewars are further perpetuated by the participants, when it
becomes plain that winning a convert is an unachievable goal, aiming
for the lesser condition of victory of "having the last word".

Anyway, I think I'm belaboring the obvious here.  The crux of what
I'm wanting to say is that once two or more folks start flaming all
parties involved in dispensing the heat tend to look equally bad
from the sidelines.

tm} (besides, my report, "Paganism and Science: The Hammer of
tm} Witches" will light a fire under this witch issue soon enough
tm} :-} ).

Awaited with anticipation in that I am one who thinks there is value
to be gained from both sides of the fence, but am unwilling to bet
my participation on it.

tm} (That's also the reason I rarely put smileys in my attempts at
tm} humor or explain myself beyond what I've already said.)

I find that in my postings to this list I am less inclined to use
smilies than I am elsewhere because of the palpable "bah, humbug
smilies" attitude.  I like to use smilies, probably because I am
almost perpetually smiling :-) To me humor is one of the
cornerstones of the universe (at least the human part of the
universe anyway :-) and if you tilt your head just a bit to the
right the world is filled with wry amusement.  So I tend to drop
smilies at places where, if this were real time conversation, I
would smile as I speak.  What I want to know is who decided that
smilies should be heads-up to the left (-:


