Subject: Word meanings and confusion

Before we consider the intial posting on this thread, "Word meanings and
confusion", as "definitive" I will offer this alternate analysis for your
consideration and clarification.

> Big O Objectivism: Objectivism (TM) as practiced by Ayn Rand, her
> personal disciples, and the fanatic level groups who would be
> considered acceptable as real Objectivists by the group she personally
> led (inherited by Piekoff on her death).

> Little o objectivism: Objectivism as practiced by people who read a
> few of her books and adopted part of her philosophy without
> internalizing the intolerance, irrationallity and self-certainty.
> (alt.objectivism ;-)

According to ~your~ definition of Big "O", I'm not one and never have
been.  Never met Ayn Rand.  Never heard of this "Piek-er" fellow you
mention.  Nope, I'm just a small-town boy from WyOmin' (not many
Objectivist discussion groups meet there ) 8^) Anyone who attempted to
coerce me in the fashion you describe would be fried in the first 5
minutes. 8^) 8^) (ask anyone who knows me, huh Mark?)

Lest we fall victim to the "Excluded Middle" trap ourselves I would
hesitate to use such a broad brush when painting your Objectivist
picture.

> [for big "0"] the preasure is strong to stop
> thinking "evil" thoughts. The only way to do this, is to learn to stop
> thinking rationally.
>     By contrast, little o objectivist groups seem to enjoy argueing
> about everything, which probably builds up the ability to think
> logically. (At least they learn to build logical sounding arguments.)

Your theory that:

	big O ==> irrational thinking, little o ==> logical thinking

does not seem to follow from your presentation.  On the contrary, it
seems that big Objectivism is more appealing to the
~exclusively-logical~ mind.  By exclusively-logical I mean logical to
the exclusion of empirical evidence.  This is the weakness in
Objectivism that Eric has pointed out.  All the big "O" ranting you
describe I suspect is unnecessary.  If a person simply applies Ayn
Rand's philosophy to its "logical" conclusion then "rock music is
evil".  Unfortunately some of her conclusions seem right-on so many
people are willing to accept her methods and premises along with the
package.

Well Ok, if you concede that:

	exclusively-logical (is the same as) irrational

then we are in agreement. 8^)

You (and others) also imply that:

	assholes (are drawn to) Big "O" Objectivism

just as

	moths (are drawn to) a flame

I would suggest that we all exibit an "AH" trait to one degree or
another and that the acceptence and practice of Objectivist
Epistomology amplifies it (this "amplification" belief seems to be
shared by Eric).  I would further suggest that someone that has
accepted ~some~ Objectivist Ideals is an "o" to one degree or another
(probably more me that Perry, unless he's into some heavy denial).
Others have suggested a distinction  between big "O" and little "o" as
EITHER the acceptance of Objectivist Epistomolgy OR the acceptence of
Objectivist politics.  If you say that you accept just the politics
then someone shouldn't even call themselves an "O" or "o"
as others have also said.  Maybe a "frogian" or something else.
(Please no flames, "frogian" is a completely ficticious name to avoid a
controversy).  The prescribed method of reading Ayn Rand is with a
checklist i.e., this I like, this is good, this stinks, forget this,
avoid these accept on Wednesdays, skip this, etc.--hardly a "useful"
philosophy then is it?  It certainly fails Occam's Razor.  For these
reasons I stand by my original claim:  "Warning:  Objectivism may be
hazardous to your health."  Or rather:  "If you must smoke
[Objectivism] then don't inhale".  I hope this was beneficial to others,
I learned a lot at the cost of several pounds of flesh. OK, so I'm not
fully rehabilitated yet, but I think I see things more clearly now. 8^)

Thanks to John McPherson and Eric S. Raymond who have once again rescued
a thread from total cathartic collapse.  (Tim May, if you deleted their
"RAND:" contributions then you certainly missed some of the best this
list has to offer)

