>PC slant. Bletch.

I agree with a lot of the statements Eric makes here, and the others
make elewhere, but a couple months ago the bio puff piece was on
Karl Popper, and was pretty good.

I guess I'd say: SciAm is pretty good if you can distinguish between
the dross and the gold; this is hard due to its higher gold to dros
ratio than other publications, which might actually make it worse than
something that's acutally bullshit.

They've also run stuff of dubious scholarship that was still interesting,
like the Mithras piece.

It was very interesting, but if they're going to have that, they should
also cover other interesting stuff w/ non-standard scolarship.

Someone talked about how their linguistics was bogus. It might have
been, but as far as the politics within the linguistic community 
(going out on a limb here) they'll probably always have "correct"
articles, tracing linguistic interaction in history by simple
root words (you know, the standard dozen) and using that as an
excuse for strict isolationism.

(For instance: I don't think he's right, but it still seems to me
that Barry Fell's stuff has been iven too little consideration, and
could one day be useful in tracing migration histories et cetera...)

(If you're a linguist and can show me you _know_ he's wrong, please get
back to me).

